{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-24dd004b-c34a-45c5-947d-e3e9de1331a6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ac23f584ad3935b5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-d9s8bc70/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n\n## Description\n\nRuns a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators to establish\nfirst-hand functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\nplatform's supported model set, and produces the per-model go/no-go scope that\ndetermines which models the ROCm serving runtime productization covers. This is\nthe strategy's risk-reduction gate: the platform ships only CUDA runtime builds\ntoday and has no first-hand ROCm parity data for its supported models.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes — procurement or partner-lab\n  engagement; the access path is unresolved at strategy sign-off and resolving\n  it is part of this epic's work.\n- Define the campaign methodology: functional correctness checks and\n  performance parity measurements versus the CUDA baseline for each model in\n  the supported model set, with explicit pass/fail criteria per model.\n- Stand up the vLLM ROCm serving stack on the acquired hardware using upstream\n  ROCm runtime images.\n- Execute the campaign across the supported model set, including multi-GPU\n  serving configurations where applicable.\n- Deliver a campaign report: per-model functional results, performance parity\n  data, and a per-model go/no-go recommendation that constitutes the\n  productization scope handed to downstream productization work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The campaign report records functional validation results (pass/fail) for\n  every model in the supported model set, measured on physical AMD Instinct\n  hardware.\n- The campaign report records performance parity measurements against the CUDA\n  baseline for every model in the supported model set.\n- Every model carries an explicit go/no-go recommendation with the criteria\n  used, and the aggregate constitutes the productization scope handed to the\n  serving runtime productization work.\n- The methodology (parity criteria, measurement procedure, hardware and software\n  configuration) is documented in the report so the results are reproducible.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware\n  establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n  supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Hardware access (procurement vs. partner lab) is unresolved and externally\n  controlled. This epic's work includes securing access, but the access being\n  granted is an availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion — the\n  deliverable this epic is judged on is the validated report.\n- Upstream ROCm runtime image releases are controlled by AMD and the upstream\n  communities; the campaign runs against the available upstream images and is\n  not blocked by release-train alignment.\n- The campaign is likely larger than one sprint of effort (functional plus\n  performance parity across the full supported model set on shared hardware);\n  it is kept as one logical change because a split would duplicate hardware\n  environment bring-up and fragment a single go/no-go report. Re-split into\n  sequential waves by model family if the campaign exceeds two sprints.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":54116,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95ba045d32a00c6b","agent_id":"aed3d9bc59117f76c","session_id":"ca1a66b0-be22-44b3-9715-c3e9690708e5","uuid":"3ee2ab08-d658-418d-8056-64c66a8382f4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:43:30.631Z","su