{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e92725ceec2b66c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001\n3\ttitle: AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n5\tcomponent: Serving Runtimes\n6\tteam: RHOAI Serving\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -2\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -1\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Low\n19\tai_implementability_score: -2\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tRuns a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct accelerators to establish\n30\tfirst-hand functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n31\tplatform's supported model set, and produces the per-model go/no-go scope that\n32\tdetermines which models the ROCm serving runtime productization covers. This is\n33\tthe strategy's risk-reduction gate: the platform ships only CUDA runtime builds\n34\ttoday and has no first-hand ROCm parity data for its supported models.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Secure access to dedicated AMD Instinct nodes — procurement or partner-lab\n39\t  engagement; the access path is unresolved at strategy sign-off and resolving\n40\t  it is part of this epic's work.\n41\t- Define the campaign methodology: functional correctness checks and\n42\t  performance parity measurements versus the CUDA baseline for each model in\n43\t  the supported model set, with explicit pass/fail criteria per model.\n44\t- Stand up the vLLM ROCm serving stack on the acquired hardware using upstream\n45\t  ROCm runtime images.\n46\t- Execute the campaign across the supported model set, including multi-GPU\n47\t  serving configurations where applicable.\n48\t- Deliver a campaign report: per-model functional results, performance parity\n49\t  data, and a per-model go/no-go recommendation that constitutes the\n50\t  productization scope handed to downstream productization work.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- The campaign report records functional validation results (pass/fail) for\n55\t  every model in the supported model set, measured on physical AMD Instinct\n56\t  hardware.\n57\t- The campaign report records performance parity measurements against the CUDA\n58\t  baseline for every model in the supported model set.\n59\t- Every model carries an explicit go/no-go recommendation with the criteria\n60\t  used, and the aggregate constitutes the productization scope handed to the\n61\t  serving runtime productization work.\n62\t- The methodology (parity criteria, measurement procedure, hardware and software\n63\t  configuration) is documented in the report so the results are reproducible.\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability\n66\t\n67\t- HLR-1 (P0): a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware\n68\t  establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the\n69\t  supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n70\t\n71\t## Risks / Notes\n72\t\n73\t- Hardware access (procurement vs. partner lab) is unresolved and externally\n74\t  controlled. This epic's work includes securing access, but the access being\n75\t  granted is an availability precondition, not an acceptance criterion — the\n76\t  deliverable this epic is judged on is the validated report.\n77\t- Upstream ROCm runtime image releases are controlled by AMD and the upstream\n78\t  communities; the campaign runs against the available upstream images and is\n79\t  not blocked by release-train alignment.\n80\t- The campaign is likely larger than one sprint of effort (functional plus\n81\t  performance parity across the full supported model set on shared hardware);\n82\t  it is kept as one logical change because a split would duplicate hardware\n83\t  environment bring-up and fragment a single go/no-go report. Re-split into\n84\t  sequential waves by model family if the campaign exceeds two sprints.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-89db8f0f5a287418","agent_id":"ab1e96f21a74eab04","session_id":"ca1a66b0-be22-44b3-9715-c3e9690708e5","uuid":"1e7ba041-2a43-488d-9db8-a7a28e98e304","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:47:03.726Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8e92725ceec2b66c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
