{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a63f51571aaf542e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\tTriage: neither below-threshold nor docs-only. The strategy spans four components\n13\t(KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator, Distributed Workloads), two owning\n14\tteams, and multiple architecturally distinct work streams (hardware validation\n15\tcampaign, serving runtime productization, training/queueing enablement,\n16\tdocumentation), with a P0 go/no-go gate that determines productization scope.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign for vLLM ROCm serving parity | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Konflux build pipeline for the vLLM ROCm runtime image | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | PyTorch training on ROCm via PyTorchJob | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Kueue admission and amd.com/gpu quota for ROCm training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | AMD ROCm enablement documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P1 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E002\n34\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E006\n35\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E006\n36\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E006\n37\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E006\n38\t    RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-9020-E006\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge (or non-edge) | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|-------------------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The validation campaign's go/no-go report determines which models the productization covers. True gate: `remove` (a full no-go removes the productization epic; a partial clear rewrites its scope to the cleared model list — the fallback approach on E002 records both branches). Recorded via `gated_by` on E002. The bounded outcomes (full clear / partial clear / no-go) change the scope of one epic, not which epics exist, so no conditional branch files are generated. |\n46\t| E001 → E006 | Rule 3 + Rule 11 context | The documentation's validated-model-list content is determined by the campaign report; the docs epic is a downstream consumer of the Investigation outcome (`gated_by` on E006, `rewrite`). |\n47\t| E002 → E006, E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy; docs describe what was built. |\n48\t| No edge E003 ↔ E002 | Rule 10 (konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation) | The Konflux pipeline work for the new ROCm image variant runs in parallel with the image build/productization work. The coupling (\"the ROCm image variant is built and released green through the Konflux pipeline\") is an acceptance-criterion gate on E002, never a DAG edge. |\n49\t| No edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rules 13/14 (configuration reference, parallel) | Training Operator job enablement and Kueue quota/admission handling are different components; the quota configuration is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact. Cross-checks are mutual acceptance criteria. |\n50\t| No edge E001 → E004 / E001 → E005 | Rule 14 (parallel streams) | The training stream (HLR-3) is independent of the serving go/no-go. Shared AMD Instinct hardware is a coordination risk (noted in epic risks), not a dependency. |\n51\t| No edge E001 → E003 | Rule 10 spirit | Pipeline onboarding is known-recipe work independent of the campaign outcome; it proceeds in parallel. |\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n56\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity for vLLM serving, producing go/no-go scope | RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | Securing hardware access (procurement or partner lab) is part of the campaign epic's scope; the access being granted is an externally-controlled precondition recorded in Risks, not an acceptance criterion. |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | RHAISTRAT-9020-E002, RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Split by work-product type (Rule 5 / Rule 20 new-container-image-in-existing-repo shape): runtime image + ServingRuntime productization vs. Konflux pipeline recipe, running in parallel per Rule 10. Same priority, not a priority split. |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single-/multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota handling | RHAISTRAT-9020-E004, RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Split by component boundary (Rule 1): Training Operator (job execution) vs. Distributed Workloads (Kueue admission/quota), both owned by the Distributed Workloads team. |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation of supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, validated model list | RHAISTRAT-9020-E006 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (not inherited from HLR-4's P2). |\n61\t\n62\tAll four HLRs are covered; no requirement is orphaned. No epic maps to HLRs at\n63\tmultiple priority levels, so no priority-split or bundling analysis applies.\n64\t\n65\t## Component Mapping Notes\n66\t\n67\t- All epic `component` values are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: Serving Runtimes, Training Operator, Distributed Workloads, Documentation.\n68\t- **KServe is passive** in this strategy: no code change is implied — InferenceServices host the new ROCm runtime. Per Step 2, since the validating team (RHOAI Serving) is the same team as the nearest active component (Serving Runtimes), KServe validation (\"InferenceServices schedule and serve correctly with the ROCm runtime and amd.com/gpu resources\") is an acceptance criterion on E002, not a separate epic.\n69\t- **Distributed Workloads is active**: HLR-3's amd.com/gpu quota handling and Kueue admission is a real change to the queueing layer, not just validation.\n70\t- **Documentation** has no architecture-context file; it is used only for the docs-authoring epic (HLR-4).\n71\t- Team assignments derive from the architecture-context owning teams (KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training Operator/Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads) because the strategy has no Impacted Teams table.\n72\t\n73\t## Health Warnings\n74\t\n75\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Staff Engineer Input. Teams were assigned from architecture-context owning teams; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs. Non-blocking; human verification recommended.\n76\t- **Hardware access (open question 1) is an availability precondition, not an AC or an epic**: procurement or partner-lab access is externally controlled and unresolved. The decision rule of Step 3 was applied: the access path does not change which epics exist or what they do (the campaign runs either way), so it is folded into the campaign epic's scope and recorded as a risk there — the acceptance criteria sit on the campaign's own deliverable (the validated parity report).\n77\t- **Upstream ROCm image cadence (open question 2) is an external dependency with uncertain timing**: AMD and the upstream communities control runtime image releases; alignment with platform release trains is outside our control. No upstream epic is created (we submit no PR/RFC); it is modeled as a precondition with tiered delivery (below) and a risk on E002/E004.\n78\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the validation campaign epic (RHAISTRAT-9020-E001) is likely above the ~2-week one-sprint guideline — functional plus performance parity across the entire supported model set on shared physical hardware. Kept as one logical change: a split would duplicate hardware environment bring-up and fragment a single go/no-go report. **Re-split trigger**: re-split the campaign into sequential waves (by model family) if the supported model set or the hardware-time budget makes the campaign exceed two sprints.\n79\t- **Scope trap watch (driver prerequisites)**: HLR-4 requires documentation of AMD GPU driver prerequisites, but no affected component covers driver or AMD GPU Operator provisioning, and the out-of-scope list excludes GPU sharing/partitioning. The docs epic states prerequisites only; platform driver provisioning is intentionally not decomposed. Flagged for human verification that this is the intent.\n80\t- **Docs epic team assumption**: the documentation epic is assigned to RHOAI Serving (serving-centric content: validated model list, runtime) though its scope touches both streams, because the strategy names no owning team for documentation.\n81\t\n82\t## Tiered Delivery\n83\t\n84\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing (upstream ROCm image release cadence) is evaluated per Step 5.5 — partial value is deliverable without it:\n85\t\n86\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**:\n87\t  - E001 — the validation campaign: risk-reduction value on its own; it runs directly against upstream ROCm images and produces the parity data and go/no-go scope regardless of release-train alignment.\n88\t  - E003 — Konflux pipeline recipe for the ROCm image variant: known-recipe work, outcome-independent.\n89\t  - E004 / E005 — the training stream (HLR-3): independent of the serving go/no-go; PyTorch ROCm images already publish upstream.\n90\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**:\n91\t  - E002 — serving runtime productization: gated by the campaign outcome (`gated_by` E001) and subject to the upstream image cadence risk (pin to the nearest aligned upstream build if trains misalign, recorded as a risk).\n92\t- **E006 (docs)** follows all implementation work per Rule 11.\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6b45b48e303b262","agent_id":"a588a9abb0bab51e1","session_id":"ca1a66b0-be22-44b3-9715-c3e9690708e5","uuid":"5bbb7098-a8a8-4945-afb1-23b83e184818","timestamp":"2026-10-26T08:53:25.715Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a63f51571aaf542e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9020 decomposition"}
